# CooperBench claim run — run log (lane 01M5GWBB17, claim-manifest.md)

## Pre-flight, 2026-10-22 (machine musterd-cooperbench, performance-8x)

- **Deploy.** First deploy at main b3017222 carried `latest` Claude Code, 2.1.308. The host
  `gate_smoke.py` failed before testing anything: "--dangerously-skip-permissions cannot be used with
  root/sudo privileges". Redeployed with Claude Code pinned to 2.1.307 (the version every pilot 3 and
  solo stream records) for the host and, through CooperBench's `setup.sh`, every agent container
  (commit 7e795fcb on this lane's branch; musterd code identical to b3017222).
- **2.1.307 refuses root too.** The pilots' agents never hit it, because CooperBench's adapter exports
  `IS_SANDBOX=1` inside each container. Only the host-side smokes run as plain root. They now run as an
  unprivileged user (`runuser -u smoke`). This touches no arm.
- **API check:** `claude -p` → ok.
- **`gate_smoke.py`** (tool search on): PASS, $0.0046. The claimed `src/a.py` was edited, the unclaimed
  `src/b.py` was refused by the gate, and there was no deaf warning.
- **`hold_smoke.py` as committed** FAILs (it predates ADR 697: it expects the post-lapse edit to be
  allowed, while 697 refuses it once as `denied_handover`), as in pilot 3. **`hold_smoke_697.py`**
  (pilot 3's copy): PASS, $0.0076. Rows: held → handover → lapsed.
- **musterd smoke pair** `pallets_click` 2068 f1+f2, `--git`: both agents Submitted, scored
  (eval_error null; both features failed tests, cause `task`), and `lookup_failures` 0 for both agents.
  Each agent ran `git fetch team` and merged its partner (partner_merges 1 each). **$2.07**, against pilot 3's
  $2.85 on the same pair (−27%). The first model call's context is 25.2k tokens (pilot 3: 39.6k).
  Tool search was on: agents loaded tools with `ToolSearch` (3 and 2 calls). The blocking wait was
  used: `team_inbox_check {wait: 45|90}` (5 and 4 calls). PASS.
- **Re-estimate** at −27% on pilot 3's $2.46 a pair: musterd ≈ $36, solo ≈ $12, smokes $2.10, about
  $50 in all, under the $60 hard stop.

## Run

- 18:12:22Z `claim.sh` started. **Aborted start:** `cooperbench prepare` takes no `-s` (it only downloads
  the dataset), so no images were pulled. The script had no `set -e`, so both arms started at 18:12:29Z and
  would have paid for image pulls in their wall time. Killed at ~18:14Z, before any pair finished
  (spend still $2.07, the smoke pair). The logs were moved to `logs/aborted-claim-*` and
  `claim-2026-10-22/aborted/`. The script now `docker pull`s every claim20 image and stops on a
  failed pull. The pilot manifest's `cooperbench prepare -s core` line was wrong in the same way.
- 18:15:40Z `claim.sh` restarted. It pulled all 20 images by 18:21:03Z, which filled the 79 GB volume
  (the 20 images are 83.4 GB). Every musterd agent container then failed its Claude Code install with
  "E: You don't have enough free space in /var/cache/apt/archives/", the arms "finished" in under 20 s,
  and **$0** was spent. These are errors, not scores. Their logs were moved to `logs/aborted2-claim-*`
  and `claim-2026-10-22/aborted2/`.
- 18:24:36Z **restarted in two halves** (`claim.sh` as committed): `core` first, then the ten new tasks
  (`claimnew`). Before each half the other half's images are removed and this half's are pulled. Both
  arms still run side by side within each half, on the same machine and day, under one $60 stop
  across both. Run names: `claim-{musterd,solo}-{core,new}`. 14 GB free at the start of the core half.
- Core half: solo 18:24:37–18:36:41Z, musterd 18:24:37–18:52:02Z; evaluated by 18:56:07Z; spent $24.47.
  No errored pair (all 20 scored; every musterd agent Submitted; zero lookup failures).
- New half: images pulled 18:57–19:01Z; solo 19:01:16–19:13:36Z, musterd 19:01:16–19:28:34Z;
  evaluated by 19:31:15Z. All done 19:31:25Z, **$49.25** model spend. No errored pair; all 20 solo
  agents and all 40 musterd agents Submitted; nothing re-run.
- Machine stopped after copying `readout.json`, `agentstats.json` (from `agentstats.py`) and `claim.log`.
